#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139664 :tutorial/content "To solve an equation with a decimal coefficient, such as\n$0.2x = -1$,\n\nwe start by getting rid of the decimal. To do this, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${\\color{blue}10}$. Multiplying a decimal by $10$ shifts the decimal place one spot over to the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.2x &= -1 \\\\\n10 \\cdot (0.2x) &= 10 \\cdot (-1) \\\\\n2x &= -10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the equation using the multiplication principle, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x &= -10 \\\\\n\\frac{2x}{2} &= \\frac{-10}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{2x}{2} &= -\\frac{10}{2} \\\\\nx &= -5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b3690d9a-5c80-54c4-b523-5aef327aa8f0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8147 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139665 :tutorial/content "How do we get rid of the decimal in an equation like\n\n$$\n0.25x = 2\n$$\n\nwhen there is more than one number behind the decimal place? If we multiply by $10$ as usual, we shift the decimal place over one spot to the right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.25x &= 2 \\\\\n10 \\cdot (0.25x) &= 10 \\cdot (2) \\\\\n2.5x &= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, there is still one number behind the decimal place. So, we can multiply by $10$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2.5x &= 20 \\\\\n10 \\cdot (2.5x) &= 10 \\cdot (20) \\\\\n25x &= 200\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that our equation contains only whole numbers, we can continue with the usual method of applying the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25x &= 200 \\\\\n\\frac{25x}{25} &= \\frac{200}{25} \\\\\n\\frac{25x}{25} &= \\frac{200}{25} \\\\\nx &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking back, we can see a faster way to apply the same method. We multiplied by $10$ twice, which means that overall, we multiplied by $100$. In general, multiplying a decimal by $100$ shifts the decimal place over two spots to the right. So, we could have solved the equation faster by multiplying by $100$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.25x &= 2 \\\\\n100 \\cdot (0.25x) &= 100 \\cdot (2) \\\\\n25x &= 200 \\\\\n\\frac{25x}{25} &= \\frac{200}{25} \\\\\n\\frac{25x}{25} &= \\frac{200}{25} \\\\\nx &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "50c650c0-be15-5aa0-87c5-181d41d15934" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8148 :tutorial/title "Solving Linear Equations with More than One Decimal Place"}]}
